+# Gate: bdd-proof
+
+*`bdd-proof` · LAW 22, LAW 53 · planes: ci, session*
+
+No pull request opens without BDD proof, across every agent session: the body carries a BDD-PROOF block naming the head commit, that block carries a runner's own passing counts rather than a sentence saying the tests passed, and the named commit is the pull request's own head -- a claim with no run output is refused, and so is a real run on another commit
+
+This page is generated by `bin/idp-gate-demo` from `rules.yaml`. The output below is what the gate itself printed when the generator ran it; it is not a description of what the gate does.
+
+## Run it yourself
+
+```bash
+python3 bin/idp-bdd-proof-gate
+```
+
+## Refused
+
+Fixture: `tests/fixtures/bdd-proof/bad.json`
+
+Exit code `1` (the registry expects non-zero). ✓
+
+```
+refused: 'platform: raise the gateway memory limit' carries no BDD-PROOF ... END-BDD-PROOF block
+```
+
+## Permitted
+
+Fixture: `tests/fixtures/bdd-proof/good.json`
+
+Exit code `0` (the registry expects zero). ✓
+
+```
+ok: bdd proof on head aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa
+```
+
+## The registry's own lines
+
+- when it passes: no pull request opens without a passing BDD run on its own head, in any agent session (LAW 22)
+- when it refuses: a pull request could open on a claim that was never proved by a BDD run (LAW 22)
+
+Origin: founder 2026-09-12
